Part A: Control plane (quixydevops)

0. Check your OS (the commands below are for Ubuntu/Debian):
cat /etc/os-release

1. Backup etcd:
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /root/etcd-backup-$(date +%F).db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

2. Switch the repo to 1.32:
sudo sed -i 's/v1\.31/v1.32/g' /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list   # confirm it says v1.32
sudo apt update
sudo apt-cache madison kubeadm

3. Upgrade kubeadm (replace x with the latest patch you saw):
sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm && \
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm='1.32.x-*' && \
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm
kubeadm version

4. Check, then apply:
sudo kubeadm upgrade plan
sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.32.x

Wait for SUCCESS! Your cluster was upgraded to "v1.32.x". kubectl may be unresponsive for a minute while the API server restarts. That’s normal with one control plane.

5. CNI: check which network plugin you use (kubectl get pods -n kube-system) and follow its upgrade guide if it requires one.

6. Drain the node:
kubectl drain quixydevops --ignore-daemonsets

7. Upgrade kubelet and kubectl:
sudo apt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl && \
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y kubelet='1.32.x-*' kubectl='1.32.x-*' && \
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
systemctl status kubelet

8. Uncordon:
kubectl uncordon quixydevops
kubectl get nodes

